<!doctype html>
{%- comment -%} settings.card_hover rides the root element rather than a class on each
card: the card snippet is rendered from eight different places (home, collection, search,
cart cross-sell, related row, quick view, complete-the-look, recently viewed) and a root
attribute means the hover rule is written once in the stylesheet instead of every caller
having to remember to pass the option through. {%- endcomment -%}
<html lang="{{ request.locale.iso_code }}" data-card-hover="{{ settings.card_hover }}">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>
    {%- if request.page_type == 'index' -%}
      {%- comment -%} Homepage: the shop's own name plus an optional merchant tagline
      (settings.seo_tagline). Never a hardcoded industry — this theme ships to every
      kind of store, and a wrong category in the <title> is what search engines index.
      A homepage SEO title equal to shop.name counts as unset, so the tagline still
      applies. {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if page_title == blank or page_title == shop.name -%}
        {{- shop.name -}}
        {%- if settings.seo_tagline != blank %} &ndash; {{ settings.seo_tagline }}{% endif -%}
      {%- else -%}
        {{- page_title -}}
        {%- unless page_title contains shop.name %} &ndash; {{ shop.name }}{% endunless -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- else -%}
      {{- page_title -}}
      {%- if current_tags %} &ndash; {{ current_tags | join: ', ' }}{% endif -%}
      {%- if current_page != 1 %} &ndash; {{ 'general.title_page' | t: page: current_page }}{% endif -%}
      {%- unless page_title contains shop.name %} &ndash; {{ shop.name }}{% endunless -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  </title>
  {%- comment -%} No generic fallback description: an unset store gets NO description tag
  rather than an invented one. The old default promised free shipping and 30-day returns
  on every store that installed the theme — a shipping/returns claim the merchant may not
  honour is a Merchant Center misrepresentation risk. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if page_description -%}
    <meta name="description" content="{{ page_description | escape }}">
  {%- elsif request.page_type == 'index' and settings.seo_description != blank -%}
    <meta name="description" content="{{ settings.seo_description | escape }}">
  {%- endif -%}
  <link rel="canonical" href="{{ canonical_url }}">
  {% render 'social-meta' %}
  {%- comment -%} brand-favicon.svg is a neutral default that ships with the theme, so this
  always resolves. The raster icons are derived from the merchant's square logo through the
  CDN instead of shipping as hand-made files: without an upload they are simply omitted,
  and the browser falls back to the SVG rather than requesting a 404. {%- endcomment -%}
  <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="{{ 'brand-favicon.svg' | asset_url }}">
  {%- if settings.brand_logo_square != blank -%}
    {%- comment -%} PNG fallback: mobile/in-app browsers + iOS don't take SVG favicons {%- endcomment -%}
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="32x32" href="{{ settings.brand_logo_square | image_url: width: 32, height: 32 }}">
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" sizes="180x180" href="{{ settings.brand_logo_square | image_url: width: 180, height: 180 }}">
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%} TYPEFACE SOURCE — decided once here, then obeyed by the preloads, the
  @font-face block and the :root stacks below.

  The theme ships Prata and Archivo as woff2 in assets/ and preloads them from this head.
  That is meaningfully faster than Shopify's font service: the files sit on the same CDN
  origin as the rest of the theme, so there is no second connection to open, and the
  preload starts the download before the stylesheet has been parsed. Handing the merchant
  a font_picker and then unconditionally emitting `| font_face` would throw that away on
  every store — including the large majority that never change the font.

  So each picker DEFAULTS to the closest Shopify-hosted equivalent of the family we ship,
  and we branch on `.family`: while a picker still reads its default family the local file
  is used, and only a genuine change to some other family moves that role onto
  `| font_face`.

  The two defaults are not symmetrical, and the reason is worth knowing before you change
  either string:

    --font-ui / --font-prose  default `archivo_n4` → family "Archivo". Exact. The picker
    names the typeface that actually renders, and a merchant who selects Archivo by hand
    gets the fast local file rather than a second download of the same typeface.

    --font-display  default `dm_serif_display_n4` → family "DM Serif Display". A STAND-IN.
    Shopify deprecated `prata_n4` and dropped Prata from the font list, so there is no
    handle that names what renders. DM Serif Display is the nearest equivalent — display
    serif, same contrast band, and single-weight like Prata rather than a variable family
    — which makes it the least misleading label available and the least costly one to
    shadow. The cost is real and is documented in the setting's own info text: a merchant
    who genuinely wants DM Serif Display gets Prata instead.

  These two strings are the contract between this file and config/settings_schema.json.
  Change a picker default there and you MUST change the matching family name here, or that
  role silently flips to Shopify-hosted for every store that never touched the setting.

  Fails safe in the direction of the shipped design: a blank, nil or unresolvable picker
  value leaves the flag false, i.e. local files. That also covers the day Shopify retires
  a handle out from under us — the storefront quietly keeps rendering Prata and Archivo
  instead of losing its font stack. There is no input that produces an empty
  font-family. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign font_display_local_family = 'DM Serif Display' -%}
  {%- assign font_body_local_family = 'Archivo' -%}
  {%- assign font_display_hosted = false -%}
  {%- assign font_body_hosted = false -%}
  {%- if settings.font_display.family != blank and settings.font_display.family != font_display_local_family -%}
    {%- assign font_display_hosted = true -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if settings.font_body.family != blank and settings.font_body.family != font_body_local_family -%}
    {%- assign font_body_hosted = true -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- unless font_display_hosted -%}
    {%- comment -%} Prata carries --font-display AND --font-accent-italic — the short
    italic tags under the product name ("in <Colour>") sit above the fold on every PDP, so
    this is a preload and not a lazy fetch. Dropped entirely once display type has moved
    to another family: nothing would reference the file. {%- endcomment -%}
    <link rel="preload" href="{{ 'prata-regular.woff2' | asset_url }}" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
  {%- endunless -%}
  {%- unless font_body_hosted -%}
    {%- comment -%} Archivo is --font-ui and --font-prose: the label system, nav, buttons,
    prices and body copy. Two files because the italic is a separate cut; the 500/600/700
    weights all ride the variable file that regular already downloads (see the
    `font-weight: 400 700` range below), so there is no third preload. {%- endcomment -%}
    <link rel="preload" href="{{ 'archivo-var.woff2' | asset_url }}" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
    <link rel="preload" href="{{ 'archivo-400i.woff2' | asset_url }}" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
  {%- endunless -%}
  {%- if template.name == 'product' and product.featured_media.media_type == 'image' -%}
    {%- comment -%} ceiling 1024w. The URLs MUST mirror the gallery's exactly (500 / 800 / 1024,
    width only, no height, no crop) — any difference and the CDN hands back a different URL than
    the gallery renders, so the hero image downloads twice. height+crop is avoided here: the
    source images are mixed formats (only 23% are true 2:3) and cropping them costs real garment
    detail. Keep in sync with sections/main-product.liquid when the PDP stage writes it. {%- endcomment -%}
    <link rel="preload" as="image"
      href="{{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 800 }}"
      imagesrcset="{{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 500 }} 500w, {{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 640 }} 640w, {{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 800 }} 800w, {{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 1024 }} 1024w"
      imagesizes="(max-width: 720px) 80vw, 46vw"
      fetchpriority="high">
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%} AMBER BUY BAR — the label colour is measured, not assumed.

  The line this replaced branched on buy_color and then returned var(--paper) from BOTH
  arms — an if/else that could only ever produce one answer, so the amber case was never
  really a case. It matters, because the shipped amber (#A88652) measures 3.19:1 on paper —
  under the WCAG AA 4.5:1 floor for the buy bar's 14px uppercase label — and 5.48:1
  against ink. The buy bar is the single most important control in the store and on an
  amber store it was shipping below AA.

  color_contrast does the arithmetic at render time instead of hardcoding the answer, so
  this stays correct for whatever amber a merchant picks: a deep bronze gets paper text
  back automatically. That is why the fix is a comparison and not a new hex default — the
  store owner's amber is kept exactly as chosen. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign amber_on_ink = settings.color_amber | color_contrast: settings.color_ink -%}
  {%- assign amber_on_paper = settings.color_amber | color_contrast: settings.color_paper -%}

  {%- comment -%} THE TOKEN BLOCK, captured once and emitted twice.

  There are two :root injections in this file (see the note above the second one): theme.css
  carries standalone fallbacks for some of these tokens and loads BETWEEN them, so a token
  that theme.css also defines has to be re-stated afterwards or the stylesheet's fallback
  wins. Rather than keep two hand-maintained copies in sync — which is how the first block
  and theme.css drifted to different --mute values — the whole set is captured here and
  printed in both places. Add a token once, get it in both. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- capture theme_tokens -%}
      --paper: {{ settings.color_paper }};
      --ink: {{ settings.color_ink }};
      --cream: {{ settings.color_cream }};
      --hairline: {{ settings.color_hairline }};
      --mute: {{ settings.color_mute }};
      {%- comment -%} Surface tones for the chapter rhythm. --limestone backs alternating
      sections and is only 1.08:1 against paper on purpose: a band that reads at arm's
      length and disappears up close. --ink-soft is the secondary PROSE voice (8.75:1 on
      paper, 8.08:1 on limestone — AAA on both), and is not interchangeable with --mute,
      which is the 14px uppercase chrome colour. {%- endcomment -%}
      --limestone: {{ settings.color_limestone }};
      --ink-soft: {{ settings.color_ink_soft }};

      {%- comment -%} --font-display is the serif display voice (.dxl/.dl/.dm — hero,
      category typo-block, editorial captions, footer newsletter headline).
      --font-ui and --font-prose are the grotesque voice (labels, buttons, nav, prose).
      --font-prose MUST be listed here even though theme.css also defines it: theme.css
      loads after the first block, so without the re-injection a merchant could change the
      body font everywhere except body copy — the one place it matters most.
      The hosted stacks take their fallbacks from Shopify's own fallback_families for the
      chosen family; the local stacks keep the metric-matched *-fallback faces defined
      below, which is what stops the swap from reflowing the hero. {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if font_display_hosted -%}
      --font-display: {{ settings.font_display.family }}, {{ settings.font_display.fallback_families }};
      {%- else -%}
      --font-display: 'Prata', 'Prata-fallback', Georgia, serif;
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if font_body_hosted -%}
      --font-ui: {{ settings.font_body.family }}, {{ settings.font_body.fallback_families }};
      --font-prose: {{ settings.font_body.family }}, {{ settings.font_body.fallback_families }};
      {%- else -%}
      --font-ui: 'Archivo', 'Archivo-fallback', 'Helvetica Neue', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
      --font-prose: 'Archivo', 'Archivo-fallback', 'Helvetica Neue', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- comment -%} The display face's small-type accent voice: it follows --font-display
      rather than --font-ui because an accent tag is type, not chrome, so moving the display
      voice moves it too.

      CURRENTLY REFERENCED BY NOTHING. It was the italic tags under the product name, but
      .pdp__color no longer exists and .pdp__descriptor / .pdp__rb-sub were both moved to
      var(--font-ui) in theme.css so the descriptor matches the title face. Kept emitted,
      and deliberately: it is one declaration, it is the documented hook for the next accent
      tag, and it is what such a tag must use rather than naming --font-display directly.
      If it is still unreferenced at the next cleanup, delete it here and in theme.css
      together — do not point a selector at it just to give it a job. {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if font_display_hosted -%}
      --font-accent-italic: {{ settings.font_display.family }}, {{ settings.font_display.fallback_families }};
      {%- else -%}
      --font-accent-italic: 'Prata', 'Prata-fallback', Georgia, serif;
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%} --type-scale multiplies the display steps as a SET, so the ratio
      between hero, heading and caption survives the change. ±8% is chosen to be one
      perceptible step without letting Generous push the 96px hero into wrapping on a
      laptop. {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- case settings.type_scale -%}
        {%- when 'compact' -%}
      --type-scale: .92;
        {%- when 'generous' -%}
      --type-scale: 1.08;
        {%- else -%}
      --type-scale: 1;
      {%- endcase -%}
      {%- comment -%} Authored in hundredths of an em so the merchant's slider shows whole
      and half numbers instead of 0.005. calc() rather than a Liquid `times: 0.01` because
      float multiplication in Liquid prints artefacts like 0.015000000000000001 — CSS does
      the arithmetic exactly. Default -0.5 reproduces the -.005em the display steps have
      always shipped with, so this setting is a no-op until it is touched. {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%} The `default:` filters on the three numeric settings are not
      belt-and-braces. An empty value here does not degrade, it emits BROKEN CSS —
      `calc( * 0.01em)` and a bare `px` — and --body-size is the font-size of every
      paragraph in the store. Shopify does supply the schema default for a key missing from
      settings_data, so this should be unreachable; it is written down anyway because the
      cost of being wrong is the whole store's body text. Liquid's `default` passes 0
      through (it only substitutes nil/false/empty), so a deliberate 0 tracking survives. {%- endcomment -%}
      --display-tracking: calc({{ settings.heading_tracking | default: -0.5 }} * 0.01em);
      --body-size: {{ settings.body_size | default: 17 }}px;

      {%- comment -%} --page is the contained measure, and it is now live: theme.css sets
      `--max: var(--page)` and `.wrap` obeys --max, so this setting governs every contained
      surface in the store from here. Because --max is a var-in-var on :root it resolves
      against :root's FINAL --page, which is the copy of this block injected after
      theme.css — so the merchant's value wins and the stylesheet's standalone fallback
      never does. Changing the default here changes the width of the whole store. {%- endcomment -%}
      --page: {{ settings.page_width | default: 1320 }}px;

      {%- comment -%} ONE spacing system, three presets. The audit's core finding was that
      this theme had no layout system at all: grids ran at a hardcoded 2px gap flush to the
      viewport edge, and every section was a flat ~48px apart, so nothing read as a chapter.
      These five tokens are that system.
        --gutter     page edge inset (also the container's padding-inline)
        --col-gap    horizontal gap inside a grid — smaller than --row-gap on purpose:
                     columns are read across, rows are read down, and equal gaps make a
                     grid read as a scatter
        --row-gap    vertical gap inside a grid, and the space a card's caption needs
        --band       space between sections — THE chapter break
        --band-tight the same break where two sections belong to one thought
      Every value is a clamp, so a preset choice never means an empty phone screen: Airy
      and Tight differ by ~2x at 1600px and much less at 390px, which is where the
      difference would otherwise hurt. {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- case settings.section_spacing -%}
        {%- when 'tight' -%}
      --gutter: clamp(16px, 3vw, 44px);
      --col-gap: 14px;
      --row-gap: 30px;
      --band: clamp(48px, 5.5vw, 88px);
      --band-tight: clamp(32px, 3.5vw, 56px);
        {%- when 'airy' -%}
      --gutter: clamp(24px, 5vw, 84px);
      --col-gap: 28px;
      --row-gap: 60px;
      --band: clamp(96px, 10.5vw, 176px);
      --band-tight: clamp(64px, 6.5vw, 112px);
        {%- else -%}
      --gutter: clamp(20px, 4vw, 64px);
      --col-gap: 20px;
      --row-gap: 44px;
      --band: clamp(72px, 8vw, 128px);
      --band-tight: clamp(48px, 5vw, 80px);
      {%- endcase -%}

      {%- comment -%} Sharp is the default and the theme's signal — square corners are what
      a serif display face and a hairline rule system are built around. Emitted as `0px`
      rather than `0` so a consumer can safely write calc(var(--card-radius) - 1px) for an
      inner edge without the calc going invalid on a unitless zero.
      Note for the stylesheet: theme.css has a hard `border-radius: 0` guardrail on inputs,
      buttons, img, dialog and fieldset. This token does not defeat it, and should not —
      apply it explicitly on the surfaces that are meant to follow the setting. {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- case settings.corner_style -%}
        {%- when 'soft' -%}
      --card-radius: 4px;
        {%- when 'round' -%}
      --card-radius: 10px;
        {%- else -%}
      --card-radius: 0px;
      {%- endcase -%}
      {%- comment -%} Written width/height for `aspect-ratio` so one crop governs every
      card in a grid — mixed source ratios were doing more to make the catalogue look
      unfinished than any single other detail. The card snippet still needs the raw
      setting to ask the CDN for a correctly proportioned image; this token is what keeps
      the BOX stable even before that image has loaded. {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- case settings.card_image_ratio -%}
        {%- when 'tall' -%}
      --card-ratio: 3 / 4;
        {%- when 'square' -%}
      --card-ratio: 1 / 1;
        {%- else -%}
      --card-ratio: 2 / 3;
      {%- endcase -%}

      {%- comment -%} The only place a warm tone may appear, and only when
      settings.buy_color selects it. {%- endcomment -%}
      --buy-fill: {% if settings.buy_color == 'amber' %}{{ settings.color_amber }}{% else %}var(--ink){% endif %};
      --buy-text: {% if settings.buy_color == 'amber' %}{% if amber_on_ink > amber_on_paper %}var(--ink){% else %}var(--paper){% endif %}{% else %}var(--paper){% endif %};
  {%- endcapture -%}
  <style>
    {%- if font_display_hosted -%}
      {%- comment -%} Shopify-hosted display face. Only regular and bold are requested:
      the display steps are all font-weight 400, and bold is emitted so a merchant whose
      chosen family has a real bold gets it rather than a synthesised one. {%- endcomment -%}
      {{ settings.font_display | font_face: font_display: 'swap' }}
      {{ settings.font_display | font_modify: 'weight', 'bold' | font_face: font_display: 'swap' }}
    {%- else -%}
    @font-face {
      font-family: 'Prata';
      src: url('{{ 'prata-regular.woff2' | asset_url }}') format('woff2');
      font-weight: 400;
      font-style: normal;
      font-display: swap;
    }
    {%- comment -%} Metric-matched local stand-in for the swap period: size-adjust and
    ascent-override are tuned so Georgia occupies the same box as Prata and the hero does
    not reflow when the real file lands. Only defined when the local file is in play — the
    hosted branch takes its fallbacks from Shopify's fallback_families instead. {%- endcomment -%}
    @font-face {
      font-family: 'Prata-fallback';
      src: local('Georgia'), local('Times New Roman');
      size-adjust: 104%;
      ascent-override: 98%;
    }
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if font_body_hosted -%}
      {%- comment -%} Shopify-hosted body face: regular, bold, italic and bold-italic, which
      is the full set this theme asks for. font_modify returns nil when the chosen family has
      no such cut and `nil | font_face` prints nothing, so a single-weight family degrades to
      browser synthesis rather than to a broken rule.
      One known cost of leaving the local Archivo: the six font-weight:600 call sites
      (.pdp__name, .pdp__rb-name, .acc__q, prose h4/strong) were relying on Archivo's
      variable axis for a true Semibold. Against a static hosted family they resolve to the
      nearest cut — 700 — which is heavier but never absent. {%- endcomment -%}
      {{ settings.font_body | font_face: font_display: 'swap' }}
      {{ settings.font_body | font_modify: 'weight', 'bold' | font_face: font_display: 'swap' }}
      {{ settings.font_body | font_modify: 'style', 'italic' | font_face: font_display: 'swap' }}
      {{ settings.font_body | font_modify: 'style', 'italic' | font_modify: 'weight', 'bold' | font_face: font_display: 'swap' }}
    {%- else -%}
    {%- comment -%} archivo-var.woff2 is the full variable file (fvar wght 100-900,
    confirmed via fontTools) but the @font-face range below is what the browser actually
    maps CSS font-weight onto — set to "400 700" so the six font-weight:600 call sites
    (.pdp__name, .pdp__rb-name, .acc__q, prose h4/strong) render as real Archivo Semibold
    instead of clamping to 500. No new file fetch: the weight is already in the download. {%- endcomment -%}
    @font-face {
      font-family: 'Archivo';
      src: url('{{ 'archivo-var.woff2' | asset_url }}') format('woff2');
      font-weight: 400 700;
      font-style: normal;
      font-display: swap;
    }
    @font-face {
      font-family: 'Archivo';
      src: url('{{ 'archivo-400i.woff2' | asset_url }}') format('woff2');
      font-weight: 400;
      font-style: italic;
      font-display: swap;
    }
    @font-face {
      font-family: 'Archivo-fallback';
      src: local('Helvetica Neue'), local('Arial');
      size-adjust: 100%;
    }
    {%- endif -%}
    :root {
{{ theme_tokens }}
    }
  </style>
  {%- comment -%} The six *-extras.css files are combined into theme.css (in their
  original load order — cascade unchanged). One stylesheet = one render-blocking
  request instead of seven. New block styles can go into a separate extras file
  linked here, then get combined into theme.css again afterward. {%- endcomment -%}
  {{ 'theme.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
  {%- comment -%} theme.css keeps standalone fallbacks in its own :root so the stylesheet
  stays readable on its own; because it loads AFTER the inline block above, those fallbacks
  would otherwise override the merchant's settings. Re-inject here, after theme.css, so the
  settings actually take effect.

  The set re-injected used to be the five colours. It is now the whole token block, because
  the tokens theme.css also declares have grown: --gutter, --body-size and --font-prose are
  all defined in theme.css:77 and would each silently ignore their new setting. Emitting the
  identical capture in both places removes the question entirely — a token added upstairs is
  a token that works, with no second list to remember.

  Worth knowing for anyone reading theme.css: this block also outranks theme.css's
  `@media (min-width: 721px) { :root { ... } }` step, because a media query adds no
  specificity and this style element comes later in the cascade. --gutter and --body-size
  are therefore no longer stepped at 721px — --gutter is a clamp that scales continuously
  instead, and --body-size is a merchant setting that should not change under them at a
  breakpoint. The other tokens in that media query (--lbl-size, --prose-size, --hdr-h,
  --ann-h) are untouched and still step. {%- endcomment -%}
  <style>
    :root {
{{ theme_tokens }}
    }
  </style>
  <script>
    /* .reveal starts at opacity 0 — scoped to html.js so a browser without
       JavaScript (or with theme.js blocked) never gets a blank page. Set here,
       in the head, so the class is present before first paint. */
    document.documentElement.classList.add('js');
    window.Theme = {
      moneyFormat: {{ shop.money_format | json }},
      /* shop.money_format always carries decimals, so every JS-repainted price
         (variant switch, cart drawer, mini view, ATC bar) printed "$240.00"
         while the server-rendered price beside it printed "$240" — the exact
         split settings.price_show_cents exists to close. money() in theme.js
         and cart-extras.js both read this flag and drop a trailing ".00" when
         it is false, which is what Liquid's money_without_trailing_zeros does.
         One switch, both halves of the store. */
      showCents: {{ settings.price_show_cents | default: false | json }},
      cartAddUrl: '{{ routes.cart_add_url }}.js',
      cartChangeUrl: '{{ routes.cart_change_url }}.js',
      cartUrl: '{{ routes.cart_url }}.js',
      searchUrl: '{{ routes.predictive_search_url }}',
      recommendationsUrl: '{{ routes.product_recommendations_url }}',
      t: {
        optionColor: {{ 'product.option_color' | t | json }},
        optionSize: {{ 'product.option_size' | t | json }},
        selectSize: {{ 'product.atc.select_size' | t | json }},
        unavailable: {{ 'product.atc.unavailable' | t | json }},
        addToBag: {{ 'product.atc.add_to_bag' | t | json }},
        soldOut: {{ 'product.atc.sold_out' | t | json }},
        selectSizeAria: {{ 'product.atc.select_size_aria' | t | json }},
        added: {{ 'product.atc.added' | t | json }},
        cartError: {{ 'cart.errors.generic' | t | json }},
        optionUnavailable: {{ 'cart.errors.option_unavailable' | t | json }},
        chooseSize: {{ 'product.complete_look.choose_size' | t | json }},
        optionSelect: {{ 'product.option_select' | t | json }},
        addShort: {{ 'cart.cross.add_short' | t | json }}
        {%- comment %} almostSoldOut/sellingFast/inStock are gone for good: they fed
        S.scarcityFor/S.applyScarcity, which dealt a stock state from product.id.
        The painters, their markup and the locale strings have all been removed. {% endcomment -%}
      }
    };
  </script>
  <script src="{{ 'theme.js' | asset_url }}" type="module"></script>
  {%- comment -%} New behaviour ships in its own module per block instead of growing theme.js,
  so multiple contributors never touch the same JS file. theme.js keeps every cart invariant
  and the PDP variant logic — these two only ADD. {%- endcomment -%}
  <script src="{{ 'pdp-extras.js' | asset_url }}" type="module"></script>
  <script src="{{ 'cart-extras.js' | asset_url }}" type="module"></script>
  {%- comment -%} swatch → card image preview; purely additive, the swatch is a
  working variant link without it {%- endcomment -%}
  <script src="{{ 'card-extras.js' | asset_url }}" type="module"></script>
  <script src="{{ 'standard-extras.js' | asset_url }}" type="module"></script>
  {%- comment -%} Rendered from the layout, not from main-product: the snippet also holds the
  Organization + WebSite blocks, which are gated on template.name == 'index'. {%- endcomment -%}
  {% render 'jsonld' %}
  {% render 'gtag' %}
  {{ content_for_header }}
  <script type="speculationrules">
    {
      "prefetch": [{
        "where": {
          "and": [
            { "href_matches": "/*" },
            { "not": { "href_matches": "/cart*" } },
            { "not": { "href_matches": "/checkout*" } },
            { "not": { "href_matches": "/account*" } }
          ]
        },
        "eagerness": "moderate"
      }]
    }
  </script>
</head>
<body class="template-{{ template.name | handle }}">
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main">{{ 'layout.skip_to_content' | t }}</a>
  {% sections 'header-group' %}
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1">
    {{ content_for_layout }}
    {%- comment -%} No PDP breadcrumb anywhere. The layout copy was removed, and the
    bottom .pdp__crumbs nav in sections/main-product.liquid was deleted along with its
    styles. Do not reintroduce either one. {%- endcomment -%}
  </main>
  {% sections 'footer-group' %}
  {%- comment -%} The floating search pill was removed. The header's search icon
  and /search are the search entry points. Do not reintroduce snippets/search-bar.liquid. {%- endcomment -%}
  {% render 'cart-drawer' %}
  {% render 'quick-view' %}
  {%- comment -%} Newsletter popup is toggle-owned via settings.newsletter_popup_enabled
  so it can be switched on or off per store. theme.js guards on #nl-popup being absent,
  so the toggle is safe. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if settings.newsletter_popup_enabled -%}
    {% render 'newsletter-popup' %}
  {%- endif -%}
  {% render 'license' %}
</body>
</html>
